- Story & Context: Hiding inside a crumbling limestone church during the midday heat, Sister Maeve confessed to the village elder that she had been secretly melting down sacred altar candles to lubricate the warped wooden keys of her old French oboe. Outside, the tropical cicadas reached a deafening pitch that mimicked the high-end frequency of a transatlantic dial-tone, drowning out her tearful apologies for letting the salt air ruin the holy paraffin. "The oil from the wax keeps the reed alive when the bottom of the sea tries to choke the song," she muttered defensively, staring at her blackened fingers.
